--- !u!114 &522777376
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 522777374}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 3ff6acaa8b9f1994499dd387e6188532, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  Comments: 'This scene shows how Animator blend trees can be used to smoothly interpolate
    between various animation states and how animation can switch between player
    input and Timeflow animation. One caveat to this method of animation is that
    it only plays back when Timeflow is playing and does not update if the time jumps
    or is scrubbed in the timeline. This is because transitions are handled by Animator
    as state changes and require continuous forward moving time to function. This
    also strictly adheres to all the blend tree logic, so it is only possible to
    animate between states that have a defined transition.


    To test player input:

    1.
    Enter play mode - Timeflow sequence plays automatically

    2. Press any of
    the keys W, S, A, D, T to trigger character animations using player input, which
    stops Timeflow playback

    3. Press space bar to resume playing the Timeflow
    sequence


    This is a simplified example and not an ideal setup, however demonstrates
    how control can be switched between player input and Timeflow animation. Another
    method could use tracks and markers to create choreographed sequences for cut
    scenes or other purposes. Which setup you use depends on the needs of your game
    or app. Or if you are only creating animation, you can solely use Timeflow and
    not implement player input.


    An empy area in the track section the TestCharacter
    object demonstrates how track sections can define active areas of animation,
    if you wish to use only animate channels at specific times.


    Also note that
    using Animator parameters cannot be combined with sequencing Animation Clips
    on the same object, which requires the Animator component to be disabled to take
    control over clip interpolation. One must either choose to use Animator blend
    trees, or to explicitly animate one clip at a time without blending.'
  Warning: 
  URLTitle: Animator Blend Trees
  URL: https://axongenesis.gitbook.io/timeflow/reference/examples/animator-blend-trees
  URL2Title: 
  URL2: 
